===Verb===
{{en-verb}}

# {{lb|en|intransitive}} To [[work]] as a [[#Noun|roustabout]].
#* {{quote-book|en|author=John Thomas (attributed)|chapter=Get Up, Jack! John, Sit Down!|editors=[[w:John Lomax|John A[very] Lomax]]; w:Alan Lomax|title=American Ballads and Folk Songs|location=New York, N.Y.|publisher=[[w:Macmillan Inc.|The Macmillan Company]]|year=1896|year_published=1934, printed 1967|page=494|pageurl=https://archive.org/details/americanballadsf00loma/page/494/mode/1up|passage=When Jack is old and weatherbeat, / Too old to '''roustabout''', / In some rum-shop they’ll let him stop, / At eight bells he’s turned out.}}
#* {{quote-journal|en|author={{w|O. Henry}} [pseudonym; William Sydney Porter]|title=“Next to Reading Matter”|journal=[[w:Everybody's Magazine|Everybody’s Magazine]]|volume=XVII|year=1907|page=593|column=1|passage=I was due to bust through that cellar door here to-night, so I hurried the rest of the way up the river, '''roustabouting''' on a lower coast packet that made a landing for every fisherman that wanted a plug of tobacco.}}
#* {{quote-journal|en|author=Vincent Wall|title=Poor Whites|journal=[[w:Saturday Review (U.S. magazine)|Saturday Review of Literature]]|date=19 March 1932|newversion=reprinted in|2ndauthor=Scott MacDonald|title2=Critical Essays on Erskine Caldwell|location2=Boston, Mass.|publisher2=[[w:G. K. Hall & Co.|G.K. Hall & Co.]]|year2=1981|page2=9|pageurl2=https://archive.org/details/criticalessayson0000unse_y8a8/page/9/mode/1up|isbn2=0-8161-8299-X|passage=Thereafter, the youth gives many years to aimless wandering about the South and the Middle West. He '''roustabouts''' with a carnival {{...}}}}

